I've discussed the issue of "lousy hit points" on several occasions. 
To recap:

1) Your god will boost max HP when you are underpowered.
2) The protection racket doesn't require strictly being at XL1
3) When you have low XLs, the monsters generated in the dungeon are
equally low level.

>and relying entirely on your pet

which is an advantage. It helps you and your pet. A XL9 pet is a
formidiable opponent against any monster generated in the upper dungeon.
The only downside is that the darned beasts stop to eat all the time.

My philosophy: Nethack is designed to bring balance in most situations.
Most actions have consequences. You see it all over: carry too much, you
slow down. Forget to eat, you starve. Pray too much, your god gets pissed.
Level up to fast, the dungeon starts to generate much tougher monsters.
The objective then is to gain advantages where you can where you can do
postive things that do not cause the game to counterpunch.

The pet is a perfect example on a few levels:

1) It can steal stuff out of shops with no consequence.
2) It can level up with no consequence.
3) The pet BUCs items when you don't know how to.

In short it can do things you cannot do without any game penalty.

Why would you not take advantage of that especially in the early game?

>for an offense while trying to scrape together enough money to buy that
>"free" AC at XL 1

Or XL2-6. It's not just an XL1 strategy, as I keep saying. And again
with the pet, there is no "money scraping". Take the time to clean out the
upper dungeon and Minetown shops and there is more than enough cash. Even
if there are absolutely no upper dungeon shops (a small chance) and only the
guaranteed minetown shops appear, shopkeeps have well of 6000 gold in their
pockets waiting to be stolen. And that's not even bothering with cleaning out
the upper level vaults that are routinely generated on DL1-4.

I've figured out that you don't really know how to run the racket. By your
description you have a XL1 character with low HP cowering after their pet,
trying to scrape up enough gold to buy a couple of measly points of
protection. It's not that way at all! You can get 15-20HP between XL1 and XL2
with just a couple of simple management tricks. The buff pet is the main
offensive weapon, but in a tight spot you simply kill low XP monsters and run
away from high XP monsters (like gnome zombies, mimics, and floating eyes for
example). Money and goods are everywhere if you simply take a few hundred
turns to clean out the shops. Why wouldn't you want to get money and goods
from the shops?

> than it is to simply kill the bad guys, level up, get
>some hit points,

Because of game balance. When you level up, the game levels up with you. And
you level up without the extra AC. And you level up without the buff pet.
That's why there are so many many early deaths to killer bees and soldier ants.
Characters level up to those monsters before they are ready to tackle them.
Trust me killer bees and soldier ants and mordor and hill orcs are no problem
to a character with a -10 AC and a XL9 pet. They don't get many hits in, and
most of the hits do minimal damage. The pet has 72 HP to suck up and can take
out the monsters in a bite or two. It simply leverages the game in the player's
direction.

You seem to be focused on HP. HP isn't the key to the game, AC, MR, reflection
and PR are. If you have -50 AC, RC, reflection, and PR you can walk around the
dungeon with 30HP and not be bothered too much. But you can have 150 HP and
a 2 AC and be dead in a couple of turns.

So get the AC down first, then level up. Buff the pet first, then level up.
A character can level up from XL2 to XL7 in about 300 turns. Levelling up is
a given. You can't help but do it. But the game levels up with the character.
So get prepped first.

That's what the protection racket is all about. Getting prepped to level up.

And I'm not saying to do it blindly or stupidly. Don't dive for the mines at
XL1 with AC5, no helmet, with a XL2 pet. That's a death warrant. Use armor to get
your AC down. Use your god and judicious levelling up to get your HP in the
25HP range. Use your pet to gather funds and to act as a buffer as you buff it
up. Enter the mines as a formidible, well prepared team ready to tackle the
mines.

> and buy your AC at XL 10 or so with the *ample* gold
>that's available in the midgame.

As ample gold isn't available in the early game? I'm usually at $2500 after
cleaning out the first shop. Plus I have all of the goods I want out of the
shop. Is it a bit boring? Yes. It does take some turns to pull off. But it's
preparation for the rest of the game. And it gets a lot faster after you've
gathered the funds from the first shop. BTW I also take considerable time
to price ID every item I come across. I also will pet BUC every piece of
armor and weaponry and try every non cursed piece.

So why pay $4000 a point when you can get it at $800 a point in the early
game?

I guess I feel so fervently about the strategy because I've worked to hone
it. Levelling up early quickly got me into situations that quickly overwhelmed my
character. By systematically running the PR in the early game, I feel like
I'm in control of the game from the first turn. Whacking jackals, and newts,
and lichens in the early game is a waste of time and game balance. Use those
to level up the pet. Then get into the mines, get the protection, then
feel prepared to tackle the middle of the game.

Again it's not high risk, low reward, when played right. Exactly the opposite.

